# vehje-runtime-gen. Design

Runtime generation is the first of the three operations the retirement of
"codegen" named: compiling a proven language definition into the runtime's own
code, once per language, at our build. This crate is its home, and the home the
third-build-locus debt demanded. Its cadence is per-language and dev-time; the
per-script wire boundary is `vehje-runtime-abi`'s, and the embedding is
`vehje-runtime-driver`'s. Keeping them separate keeps the language-build
machinery out of the crate whose identity is a pure wire surface, and gives the
manifest (the no-trusted-emitter mechanism) and the wire format (the
untrusted-load surface) their different trust stories separate homes.

The composition rule is the crate's discipline: it does not define any data
slice. Each slice's format is owned by the stage crate that produces it (the
family table by `vehje-ir` and `vehje-signature`, the effect masks and lease-rule
schema by `vehje-check`, the cheap-lowering rule table by `vehje-lower`, the wire
layout by `vehje-runtime-abi`). This crate composes those slices into one
package and binds it, so there is one definition per mechanism and one
aggregation point, which is how the taxonomy stays honest about the two-artifact
model.

## The validated-data package

- `Package`. The composed per-language artifact: the family table, the effect
  masks, the lease-rule schema, the binding-time defaults, the cheap-lowering
  rule table, the wire layout, and a reserved stencil section (whose schema
  exists from day one and whose producer is the deferred stencil toolchain).
  The package is the data the Zig runtime's comptime specialisation reads; it is
  data, not runtime source.
- `Slice`. The typed handle to one stage crate's contribution, so the package
  composes slices without knowing their internal format, and a stage crate's
  format change does not ripple here.
- `SliceKind`. The kind tag on a `Slice`, naming which validated-data section
  the slice carries (the family table, the effect masks, the lease-rule schema,
  and so on), so the package composes and addresses its slices by kind.

## The content-addressed manifest

- `Manifest`. The binding between the package and the runtime: a
  content-addressed hash (over `vehje-ir`'s one structural-hash primitive) of
  each slice and of the whole package, so the specialised runtime can prove at
  its own build that it was specialised from exactly this package. This is the
  cheaper manifest-check interim of the lens-projection handoff; the full lens
  is the open handoff-staging question and lands here when it lands.
- `generate`. Compiles a `Signature` (plus the stage slices) into a `Package`
  and its `Manifest`. The entry the dev-time language compiler calls to produce
  the language artifact.

## The differential-check contract

- `DifferentialCheck`. The contract for the differential harness that checks the
  specialised runtime computes what the reference does over a census corpus,
  the grade-0 operational shadow of the assurance-indexed logical relation. The
  proof document strengthens this existing seam rather than opening a new one;
  the harness itself is the contract this crate owns.

## The three loci and the Zig side

The third build locus is a build-pipeline fact about the Zig artifact. This
crate owns the entire Rust side of it: the package format, the manifest, and the
differential-check contract. The Zig side (the `build.zig` step running the
identical kernel against the package and manifest for content validation) lives
in the repo-level runtime tree, not here. No Rust crate is the third locus; this
crate is its emitter and binding contract.

## Non-scope

- Defining any data slice (the stage crates do).
- The per-script wire format (`vehje-runtime-abi`) and the embedding
  (`vehje-runtime-driver`).
- The Zig runtime source and its `build.zig` (repo-level, not a crate).

## Dependencies

`vehje-signature` (the language definition it compiles), `vehje-check`,
`vehje-lower`, `vehje-runtime-abi` (the stage slices it composes), `vehje-ir`
(the structural hash), and `notko`. `#![no_std]`, no alloc.
